    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M8 V22: Particle accelerators. How do accelerators create and test evidence about particles? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "There are two reasons. First, to make a heavy particle, the collision must supply at least its mass as energy, E equals m c squared. A Higgs boson needs at least a hundred and twenty-five GeV. Second, seeing tiny structure needs a tiny wavelength, so huge momentum. So the collision energy decides which particles and processes are possible. You speed particles up with electric fields. A charge crossing a potential difference gains energy, W equals q V. A proton crossing one million volts gains one MeV. Reaching the biggest collider's energy in one go would take almost seven million million volts. So accelerators give many smaller pushes, one after another. The simplest way is a straight line, a linear accelerator. Particles pass through a row of gaps. The fields in the gaps flip in step, so every time a particle reaches a gap, it's pushed forwards. Each gap adds a little more energy. But a straight machine can only be so long. The one in California was three kilometres long, and that limits the energy it can reach.",
    "conversationalApproach": "Most of the Standard Model never turns up in ordinary matter. So how do you make a Higgs boson, or a top quark, to study it? Make your prediction. You smash particles together, with enormous energy. So how do accelerators create and test evidence about particles? There are two reasons. First, to make a heavy particle, the collision must supply at least its mass as energy, E equals m c squared. A Higgs boson needs at least a hundred and twenty-five GeV. Second, seeing tiny structure needs a tiny wavelength, so huge momentum. So the collision energy decides which particles and processes are possible. You speed particles up with electric fields. A charge crossing a potential difference gains energy, W equals q V. A proton crossing one million volts gains one MeV. Reaching the biggest collider's energy in one go would take almost seven million million volts. So accelerators give many smaller pushes, one after another.",
    "openingDecision": "opens on v21's final frame (see \"Opening frame\"). On \"Most of the Standard Model never turns up in ordinary matter\" the gold \"made in collisions\" tags pulse. On \"a Higgs boson, or a top quark\" the H and t cells enlarge. On \"to study it\" the painted CMS-style detector `y12p-cms-detector-v1.png` slides in at the right (solid, about 560 px wide), gold hand tag \"made and seen here\" with a gold pen arrow from the H cell to the detector. On \"prediction\" a 3 s `Countdown` beside chips \"heat something\", \"smash particles together very fast\", \"find one in rocks\". On \"You smash particles together\" the second chip ticks; the ring behind the table brightens, its two dots meeting in a flash. On \"particles\" the chip becomes the heading: \"How do accelerators create and test evidence about particles?\".",
    "understandingCheck": "Most of the Standard Model never turns up in ordinary matter. So how do you make a Higgs boson, or a top quark, to study it? Make your prediction. You smash particles together, with enormous energy. So how do accelerators create and test evidence about particles? Your turn. In the Large Hadron Collider, protons with the momentum given travel round a bend of radius two point eight zero kilometres. Calculate the magnetic field the bending magnets need. Pause here to answer. Last one. Explain the roles of the electric and magnetic fields in a synchrotron, and why the magnetic field must increase as the particles speed up. Pause here to answer.",
    "curriculumScope": "# Year 12 Physics M8 V22: Particle accelerators. How do accelerators create and test evidence about particles? (design)\n\nStatus: Revision 3 (13 Nov 2026): visual-standard top-up (stage list, question-card pictures, givens strips, five-line notes, simulation engines named, root rules R1 to R4), visuals only. Narration unchanged. Needs a Codex error check before plates and voicing. Previously Revision 2 (7 Nov 2026): brief 193 findings resolved and visual upgrade to maths-visual-standard.md.\n\nNarration 6,432 characters, 21 scenes (18 teaching scenes, the notes hold, the course-end scene and a silent end hold), about 557 s, counted by script with the build parser rules (total = sum of scene strings; see \"Narration length estimate\"). Opens on v21's final frame. This is the last video of Year 12 Physics; it ends on a course-end frame.\n\nScope: twenty-second and final video of Year 12 Physics Module 8 (From the Universe to the Atom, Physics Stage 6, 2017 syllabus), inquiry question \"How is it known that human understanding of matter is still incomplete?\". Templates as `phys-y12-m8-v05.md`; `math-a12-d10.md` for the calculation layout. No pre-design pack exists; the pack work is done here (2017 wording from the ledger, site values kept, every number recalculated in Python with HSC data-sheet constants, original attempts, asset reuse). Site lesson read in full (6 Nov, read-only, through the brief 168 text extract `work/brief168/module8-lesson19.sa.txt`, hash checked against the live file):\n- `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module8/lesson19.sa.html`, SHA256 `8449bbae72ba25f4d301851cfd6740cb1fdbd52e7e539686844ba7ebe0332f7a` (matches the route): orient and predict (13.6 TeV; why rings; how short-lived particles are seen), compare linacs and synchrotrons (SLAC 1967; why high energy; linacs; synchrotrons with RF cavities, dipoles, quadrupoles, ramped field; LHC 27 km, 6.8 TeV per beam; colliders against fixed targets; luminosity; Figure 1), read the detector layers (tracking, EM and hadronic calorimeters, muon detectors, neutrinos as missing momentum; Figure 2; marked extension), clear the misconceptions (beam energy against centre-of-mass energy; photons leave no track; no neutrino layer), calculate collision energies, read detector signatures (\"Analyse 7 marks\": electric and magnetic fields in a synchrotron; \"Assess 6 marks\": the role of accelerators in testing the Standard Model), independent practice, retrieve and reflect.\n\nRoute entry: `phys-y12-m8-v22`, sections `compare-linacs-and-synchrotrons`, `read-the-detector-layers`, `read-detector-signatures`. Route start: \"Start from the physical case in the learner question; recall only the earlier result needed for it.\" Route stop: \"Explain electric acceleration, magnetic steering/focusing and detector evidence, linking collision energy to accessible processes. Stop before full RF or detector engineering.\" Next: none (\"Consolidate experimental evidence and selected exam applications; no new compulsory content.\"). Entry knowledge asks that each newly introduced equation is derived before use: B = p/(qr) is rearranged on screen from r = p/(qB), m6-v05's r = mv/(qB) written with momentum p (Revision 2, brief 193: p = mv is the low-speed case; at these energies p is the relativistic momentum, given on the cards); W = qV is recalled from m6-v03.\n\nOwner rules applied: 2017 syllabus quoted exactly; no people, hands, faces or body outlines (detector and tunnel plates contain no figures for scale); the linac tunnel (reused from v20), an accelerator dipole magnet and the CMS detector cutaway (new) are painted plates, scaled uniformly, never stretched; accelerator paths, collision products and detector signatures are coded and every event display is labelled \"schematic, not real data\" (kit teaching reason for lesson 19: \"no generated collision image is evidence\"); every number recalculated in Python with e = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ (data sheet); \"Pause here to answer.\" with a 10 s countdown; brief feedback with one crossed-out mistake; original attempts; Australian spelling; no em dashes. Recalled, not re-taught: m6-v03 (W = qV sets a particle's kinetic energy), m6-v05 (r = mv/(qB); magnetic force does no work), v20 (scattering needs short wavelength; SLAC), v21 (the Standard Model table, the Higgs), m7-v16 (E = mc²), Year 11 (momentum conservation), v19 (tokamak circles), v18 (OPAL, for the course-end montage).\n\nSyllabus content (Physics Stage 6 (2017), Module 8; ledger id `P8-IQ5-03`), quoted from the ledger `docs/production/programme/syllabus/phys-2017-y12.json`:\n\n- `P8-IQ5-03`: \"investigate the operation and role of particle accelerators in obtaining evidence that tests and/or validates aspects of theories, including the Standard Model of matter (ACSPH120, ACSPH121, ACSPH122, ACSPH146)\"\n\nSource: https://www.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/noindex/2025-03/physics-stage-6-syllabus-2017.docx (page 64)\n\n**Boundary:** starts on v21's final frame (the full Standard Model table with most cells tagged \"made in collisions\", a ring with two counter-circulating dots behind it). Teaches: why accelerators need high energy (E = mc² to create heavy particles; short wavelength to see small structure); electric fields accelerate (W = qV) and why one huge voltage is impossible, so many small pushes; the linear accelerator (gaps whose field flips in step) and its length limit; why bend into a ring (reuse the same pushes each lap), with magnetic fields to steer (no work, m6-v05) and to focus; the synchrotron condition (fixed radius, so B rises with momentum: B = p/(qr)); the Australian Synchrotron booster worked example (3.0 GeV electrons, r = 8.0 m, B = 1.25 T; at injection 0.042 T); electrons radiate when bent (synchrotron light: the Australian Synchrotron's purpose; why protons for the highest energies); the LHC (27 km, 6.8 TeV per beam, head-on collisions, 13.6 TeV available); detector layers and particle signatures, including the neutrino by missing momentum; the Higgs test (predicted, found at 125 GeV in 2012, a test that could have failed), W and Z in 1983; the limits of accelerator evidence; a short Year 12 course close. Stops before RF cavity design, magnet engineering, luminosity, invariant-mass formalism and centre-of-mass formulae (grey tags only).\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **Learner question (route wording kept).** \"How do accelerators create and test evidence about particles?\" The answer: they give particles enormous kinetic energy using electric fields, many times over (3, 4, 6), and steer and focus them with magnetic fields so they can be reused and collided (5 to 9); in a collision that energy becomes new, heavy particles (E = mc²) or probes tiny structure (2); layered detectors read what comes out (10, 11); the theory predicts what should appear, at what energy, so a result could have failed it (12), within limits (13).\n- **Hook (v21's handoff).** 3 s prediction chips for \"How do you make a Higgs boson?\": \"heat something\", \"smash particles together very fast\", \"find one in rocks\". Answer: smash particles together with enough energy.\n- **Why high energy (site card 1; m7-v16 and v20 recalled).** Two reasons, both from the site: creating a heavy particle needs at least its mc² in the collision (Higgs 125 GeV, top 173 GeV; Revision 2, brief 193: this is a necessary minimum, not a sufficient threshold, because conservation laws usually require other particles to be made too; the scene 2 slider is a rest-energy comparison, not cells \"switching on\"); seeing small structure needs short wavelength (v20). \"Collision energy decides which particles and processes are possible\" (route).\n- **Electric acceleration (m6-v03; site \"Analyse 7 marks\" step 2).** W = qV: a proton across 1 MV gains 1 MeV; 6.8 TeV in one step would need 6.8 × 10¹² V, impossible, so accelerators give many small pushes. Said \"almost seven million million volts\".\n- **Linac (site).** A row of gaps; the field in each gap flips in step with the particle's arrival, so it is always pushed forwards and shielded when the field is backwards (drift tubes, named on screen). Limited by length: SLAC 3 km, 50 GeV electrons (site). The v20 linac plate reused. The Australian Synchrotron's own linac (100 MeV) is named in scene 7's chain (sourced).\n- **Ring (site \"Analyse 7 marks\" steps 1, 4).** Magnetic force is perpendicular to the velocity, so it does no work and only steers (m6-v05 recalled); a ring brings the particle back to the same accelerating gap each lap. \"Electric fields push, magnetic fields steer\" is the organising contrast. Focusing: other magnets squeeze the beam so it stays narrow (quadrupoles named on screen; route \"focusing\"; no engineering).\n- **Synchrotron condition (site steps 3, 5; m6-v05 rearranged).** Revision 2 (brief 193): the narration starts from r = p/(qB), with p the momentum (\"At low speeds that's m v\"), so for a fixed radius B = p/(qr): as the momentum (and energy) grows, the field must grow in step, and the accelerating field's timing too; \"that's where the name comes from\" (site step 3). For particles near the speed of light the momentum is given in the question (the relativistic p is beyond this video; author note a). Momentum and energy, not speed alone, are the ramped quantities.\n- **Worked example (Australian; sourced).** Australian Synchrotron, Clayton, Melbourne (ANSTO, operating since 2007): a 100 MeV linac feeds a 130 m booster synchrotron that takes electrons to 3.0 GeV, then a 216 m storage ring. Booster at 3.0 GeV: p = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁸ kg m s⁻¹, r = 8.0 m, B = p/(qr) = 1.25 T (matches the published booster dipole field 1.25 T); at injection (0.1 GeV), B = 0.042 T, so the field ramps about 30 times during each cycle. Sources: World Nuclear Association, https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/appendices/australian-research-reactors (3 GeV, 216 m, 130 m, since 2007); ANSTO, https://www.ansto.gov.au/accelerator-systems-overview (100 MeV linac, booster); booster dipole 1.25 T and 8.25° bends of 1.15 m (bending radius 8.0 m): EPAC/PAC booster comparison, https://epaper.kek.jp/p07/PAPERS/TUPMN001.PDF . Brief 193 confirmed the strong-dipole 1.25 T and 7.99 m bending radius from the booster design paper, tables 1 and 2 (https://proceedings.jacow.org/p05/PAPERS/RPAE084.PDF); the 8 m is the bend radius, not the ring's average radius (open decision 2 closed).\n- **Synchrotron light (site step 6).** A charge forced round a curve radiates; electrons, being light, radiate strongly; the Australian Synchrotron keeps electrons circling in its storage ring precisely to make that light (intense X-rays for studying materials and proteins); the same loss is why the highest-energy collider uses protons. Named and explained in one scene; the E⁴/m⁴ scaling is author note b.\n- **LHC and colliders (site).** 27 km ring at CERN; protons at 6.8 TeV per beam; about 8 T superconducting dipoles (author note c); two beams head-on, so 13.6 TeV is available (site misconception: beam energy against collision energy). Fixed target: much of the energy is wasted carrying products forwards (site); the formula and 113 GeV appear as a grey \"extension\" tag only.\n- **Detectors (site `read-the-detector-layers`, marked extension by the site but in the route's sections).** One detector chosen (CMS, as the image-bank brief requires: \"Choose one, not a hybrid\"): from the beam outwards: tracker inside a strong magnetic field (curved tracks: radius gives momentum, direction of bending gives the sign of charge; neutral particles leave no track), electromagnetic calorimeter (stops electrons and photons), hadron calorimeter (stops protons, neutrons, pions), muon chambers (muons reach them and leave outer tracks). Neutrinos escape; Revision 2 (brief 193): missing transverse momentum shows that something unseen carried momentum, with a neutrino as one physical source, not a unique identification (\"such as a neutrino\"); muons are the charged particles that leave tracks in the outer chambers (not \"only muons get through\", since neutrinos also pass through unseen).\n- **Testing the Standard Model (site \"Assess 6 marks\").** The Higgs: predicted in 1964 (v21), sought for decades, found in 2012 by two separate detectors as an excess of photon pairs with a combined (invariant) mass of 125 GeV (site: H → γγ, H → ZZ* → 4ℓ). Revision 2 (brief 193): the histogram axis is \"two-photon (invariant) mass\", set by both photons' energies and the angle between them, not the plain sum of their lab energies. The W and Z found at CERN in 1983 at the predicted masses (site lesson 18). Falsifiability: \"if it hadn't turned up at all, the theory would have been in trouble\". Limits (site step 5): energy reach; indirect evidence from decay products; gravity outside the Standard Model. Revision 2 (brief 193): the narration no longer says \"no accelerator can test gravity\" (accelerator-made antihydrogen was used by ALPHA to test gravitational free fall); it says gravity sits outside the Standard Model, so testing the model says nothing about it. Bounded verdict (site step 6).\n- **Memory aid (scene 14, new): \"Electric fields push. Magnetic fields steer. Detectors read the debris.\"** See \"Memory aid check\".\n- **Attempts (original, HSC style).** A1 (3 marks): LHC dipole field for p = 3.63 × 10⁻¹⁵ kg m s⁻¹ protons on r = 2.80 km: 8.09 T. Crossed: leaving r in kilometres (8090 T). A2 (3 marks, explain): the roles of electric and magnetic fields in a synchrotron, and why the magnetic field increases. Crossed: \"the magnetic field speeds the particles up\".\n- **Course end (brief's instruction).** After the key notes, a short course-end scene (about 25 s of narration and a 3 s hold) closes Year 12 Physics: one thumbnail per module (M5 projectile and orbit, M6 a charge circling in a field, M7 a light wave and spectrum, M8 the atom and the Standard Model table), the line \"Year 12 Physics: complete\", and \"Next: practise with past HSC papers\". No new content (route `nextHandoff`). See \"Final frame\".\n- **Colour key (as v20 and v21):** protons rose dots, electrons navy dots, photons amber wavy arrows, muons navy dots with a long tail, neutrinos grey dashed (invisible to the detector), hadrons rose clusters; electric field arrows amber, magnetic field cross grids navy; detector layers: tracker pale grey, EM calorimeter green, hadron calorimeter orange, muon chambers blue-grey (CMS public colour convention, simplified); event displays on a pale grey card with \"schematic, not real data\".\n- **Colour key (Revision 2, fixed for every scene).** As above; energy and momentum readouts slate; B readouts navy; ticks green; framed answers in a dark-ink box; crossed chips and their hand tags red. Hand-font tags take the colour of the thing they mark (electric-field tags amber, magnetic tags navy, particle tags by their dot colour, detector-layer tags by their layer colour).\n- **Type sizes (Revision 2, 1080p).** Current working line at least 44 px (scenes 6, 7, 16 and the feedback rows 52 px; framed answers 56 px); live readouts (scene 2 collision energy, scene 3 energy, scene 6 energy, p and B, scene 4 energy per gap) about 52 px; layer and ring labels 30 to 36 px; hand-font tags at least 30 px; question text 36 px; key notes 34 px; course-end labels 36 px; nothing a student must read below 26 px. All text above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Anchor (Revision 2).** The delivered painted CMS-style detector `y12p-cms-detector-v1.png` (inspected 7 Nov: a cutaway barrel detector, layered shells around a beam pipe; no text or people) slides in on scene 1's \"to study it\" as where a Higgs is made and seen, and carries scene 10. The delivered dipole magnet `y12p-dipole-magnet-v1.png` (inspected 7 Nov: a long blue cryostat on stands, the near end cut away to two beam pipes in copper-coloured coils and a steel yoke; no text) carries scenes 5 and 9 and the A1 card; the linac tunnel carries scene 4. The opening frame is v21's coded final frame.\n- **Simulation (Revision 2).** The core live control is the field ramp in scene 6: an energy slider steps 0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0 GeV with live readouts (52 px) of p and B at fixed r = 8.0 m (Python: p = 5.34 × 10⁻²⁰ to 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁸ kg m s⁻¹; B = 0.042, 0.21, 0.42, 0.83, 1.25 T) while the orbit stays on the ring. Scene 2's collision-energy slider (a rest-energy comparison), scene 3's voltage staircase and scene 4's per-gap energy readout are the other live elements.\n- **Board stages (Revision 2).** No continued set-up runs past about 64 s (the detector layers into the signatures, 10 to 11); every stage opens with its main picture solid.\n- **Question cards (Revision 2).** Each card arrives with its full text and a picture in one move (A1: the painted dipole magnet with a coded curved beam arrow tagged \"bend radius 2.80 km\"; A2: the coded ring with one glowing gap and navy magnet segments, no force arrows). On the answer side the card shrinks to the top, a givens strip lists the data or the command, the picture stays, results are framed and one classic slip is crossed.\n- **Site sources used:** lesson 19 card 1 (why high energy; linacs; synchrotrons; dipoles and quadrupoles; ramped field; LHC 27 km, 6.8 TeV, 13.6 TeV; colliders against fixed targets; Figure 1) (2 to 9); card 2 (detector layers; signatures; neutrinos; Figure 2) (10, 11); misconceptions (13.6 not 6.8 TeV; photons leave no track; no neutrino layer) (9, 11); HSC tip (Higgs via γγ and 4ℓ) (12); \"Analyse 7 marks\" (division of labour; F = qE does work; alternating field in step; magnetic force as centripetal; ramped field; synchrotron radiation; purpose) (3 to 8, A2); \"Assess 6 marks\" (what testing requires; two kinds of evidence; falsifiability; three limits; bounded verdict) (12, 13); Q1(c) method (B from p = qBr) (A1). Not used: luminosity, √s formulae and invariant mass (grey tags only); the 1 GeV/c = 5.34 × 10⁻¹⁹ kg m s⁻¹ conversion (momenta are given in SI).\n- **Site notes (quoted from the site, read 6 Nov; for root to log in `../site-corrections-phys-y12.md` if accepted):**\n  1. SLAC length (lesson 19): card 1 says \"SLAC reached 50 GeV electrons over a 3 km track\"; retrieve and reflect says \"SLAC's 3.2 km linear accelerator\". The linac is 3.2 km (2 miles); 50 GeV was reached in its later SLC upgrade (1989), while the 1967 experiments ran at up to about 20 GeV. Suggest \"3.2 km\" in both places and \"later reached 50 GeV\".\n  2. Detector field ordering (lesson 19 card 2): \"A strong magnetic field bends their trajectories\" in the tracker; in CMS the solenoid encloses the tracker and both calorimeters, while in ATLAS the solenoid is inside the calorimeters; the generic statement is fine, but Figure 2 should not be read as one specific detector. Note only.\n  3. Higgs channel wording (lesson 19 independent practice): \"two photon showers in the EM calorimeter with no inner tracks\" is correct for unconverted photons; about a third of photons convert to electron pairs in the tracker. Note only.\n  4. Checked and correct: LHC 27 km (26.7), 6.8 TeV per beam, 13.6 TeV; Higgs 125 GeV, 2012; W 1983; fixed-target 400 GeV example √(2 × 0.938 × 400) = 27.4 GeV; 6.8 TeV fixed target 113 GeV; Q1(c) r = 16.7 m; dipoles steer, RF cavities accelerate; synchrotron radiation limits electrons.\n- **Cause chain taught, in order:** (1) heavy particles need at least mc² of collision energy, and small structure needs short wavelength (2); (2) an electric field does work qV, but one huge voltage is impossible, so many pushes (3); (3) a linac adds pushes in a line, timed so each one is forwards, and is limited by length (4); (4) a magnetic force is perpendicular to the motion, so it steers without doing work, and a ring reuses the pushes (5); (5) with a fixed radius, the field must rise as momentum rises, B = p/(qr) (6, 7); (6) bending makes electrons radiate light, useful at the Australian Synchrotron and a limit for colliders (8); (7) colliding two beams head-on makes all their energy available (9); (8) layered detectors identify particles by where they stop and how they bend; missing momentum reveals something unseen, such as neutrinos (10, 11); (9) the theory predicted what should appear and where, so finding it at 125 GeV was a test it could have failed (12); (10) the evidence has limits (13).\n- **Entry knowledge (route `entryKnowledge`):** v21 (the table, the Higgs); v20 (SLAC, wavelength); m6-v03 (W = qV); m6-v05 (r = mv/(qB), zero work); m7-v16 (E = mc²); Year 11 (momentum conservation).\n- **Learner-visible vocabulary (plain meaning at first use):** particle accelerator (a machine that gives charged particles huge kinetic energy, 1); linear accelerator (linac) (4); drift tube (screen only, 4); synchrotron (a ring accelerator whose magnetic field rises in step with the particles' momentum, 6); dipole and quadrupole magnets (bending and focusing, 5); synchrotron light (8); collider (9); tracker, calorimeter, muon chamber (10); missing momentum (11).\n- **Legacy logic ported:** none. `m8-accelerator` supplies the ring and the layered detector schematic (5 to 11) within its limits (\"Ring not an LHC engineering layout; no timing or magnet ramp model\"; \"Detector trajectories are schematic; no quantitative momentum reconstruction or missing-momentum inference\"): this design adds the ramp readout and the missing-momentum arrow as new coded parts.\n- **New explainer motion:** **voltage ladder** (a single huge voltage bar crossed, then a staircase of small steps); **linac gaps** (a row of drift tubes with field arrows that flip as a particle dot passes, its speed bar rising step by step); **ring push and steer** (a ring with one accelerating gap glowing amber and magnet segments navy; force arrows: amber along the motion in the gap, navy towards the centre in the magnets); **field ramp** (an energy slider with live p and B readouts tracking it, the orbit radius readout fixed; Revision 2); **synchrotron light** (amber light cones leaving tangentially from an electron's curved path); **head-on collision** (two dots meeting with an energy bar summing); **detector layers** (`m8-accelerator`, upgraded: concentric layers with schematic tracks, deposits and a missing-momentum arrow); **two-photon bump** (a schematic histogram of two-photon (invariant) mass with a smooth background and a small bump at 125 GeV, labelled \"schematic, not real data\"); `AlignedAlgebra`; `NumberTicker`; `Countdown` (3 s in scene 1; 10 s in scenes 15 and 17); `QuestionCard`; `Chip` with `cross`; `KeyNotes`; `Heading`.\n- **Board layout:** heading top-left; the machine or detector view in the centre; equations and readouts at the right; everything above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Author notes (not learner copy):**\n  (a) Momenta: for a 3.0 GeV electron, γ ≈ 5,900, so p = E/c to better than one part in 10⁷: 3.0 × 10⁹ × 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ / 3.00 × 10⁸ = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁸ kg m s⁻¹. For a 6.8 TeV proton, p = 3.63 × 10⁻¹⁵ kg m s⁻¹. B = p/(qr) holds relativistically with relativistic momentum (the formula sheet's p = m₀v/√(1 − v²/c²)); the cards give p directly.\n  (b) Synchrotron radiation energy loss per turn ∝ E⁴/(m⁴r): at equal energy and radius an electron loses (1836)⁴ ≈ 1.1 × 10¹³ times more than a proton.\n  (c) LHC: circumference 26.7 km; 1232 superconducting dipoles, 8.33 T design at 7 TeV; at 6.8 TeV about 8.1 T; bending radius 2804 m (the ring includes straight sections, so 27 km / 2π overstates it). Run 3 at 13.6 TeV began in 2022.\n  (d) CMS: superconducting solenoid 3.8 T enclosing tracker, ECAL (lead tungstate crystals) and HCAL; muon chambers interleaved with the iron return yoke. ATLAS differs (inner solenoid, outer toroids); the design shows CMS only.\n  (e) Higgs: predicted 1964 (Brout and Englert; Higgs; Guralnik, Hagen and Kibble); announced 4 July 2012 by ATLAS and CMS. W and Z: UA1 and UA2 at CERN, 1983.\n  (f) Australian Synchrotron facts: Clayton, Victoria; operated by ANSTO; first light 2006, user operations 2007; 100 MeV linac, 130 m booster (0.1 to 3.0 GeV, dipole 1.25 T at 3 GeV), 216 m storage ring at 3.0 GeV; beamlines include macromolecular crystallography and medical imaging.\n  (h) Revision 2 (brief 193): momentum. At these energies p is the relativistic momentum; r = p/(qB) holds with it, and p = mv only at low speeds (scene 6 says so). Energy and momentum, not speed, are what ramp (the speed barely changes near c).\n  (i) Revision 2 (brief 193): the Higgs histogram's axis is the diphoton invariant mass, m = √(2E₁E₂(1 − cos θ))/c² for massless photons, from both energies and their opening angle (ATLAS 2012, CDS 1460410).\n  (j) Revision 2 (brief 193): missing transverse momentum identifies undetected momentum; neutrinos are one source (hypothetical new particles are another). Muons leave tracks in the outer chambers; neutrinos also pass through every layer, unseen.\n  (k) Revision 2 (brief 193): ALPHA at CERN measured the gravitational free fall of antihydrogen made from accelerator-produced antiprotons (2023), so accelerators can contribute to gravity tests; the valid limitation is that gravity is outside the Standard Model.\n  (l) Revision 2 (brief 193): a collision energy at or above a particle's mc² is necessary, not sufficient; conservation of charge, baryon and lepton number and momentum usually require other particles in the final state. The scene 2 slider is a rest-energy comparison.\n  (g) Pronunciations: \"linac\" as \"lin-ack\", \"synchrotron\" as \"sink-row-tron\", \"calorimeter\" as \"kal-or-im-it-er\", \"CERN\" as \"sern\", \"CMS\" as \"C M S\", \"TeV\" as \"tera-electron-volts\" (narration says \"trillion electron volts\").\n- **Open decisions for root:**\n  - **1. Detector depth (root).** The site marks detector layers as extension; the route lists them. Kept to two scenes (10, 11) with no calculation. Confirm.\n  - **2. Boo